  "matches": [],
  "answer": "I couldn't find an earlier report of this problem. Peek.UI.exe doesn't check that the runner PID passed on its command line belongs to a running process. When you give it a PID that doesn't exist, such as 9999999, it starts with no UI and keeps running as a zombie process. Peek is meant to be launched by the PowerToys runner, not by hand. Until this is fixed, don't start PowerToys.Peek.UI.exe yourself with an arbitrary argument. If a stray instance is already running, end it in Task Manager. We will track this as a new bug: Peek should validate the PID and exit with a logged error when the process doesn't exist."
 },
